[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Read the reports instead of the
subjects and it is three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not known. Three problems, one ticket each. Fixing the parser leaves the other two open.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first; every tenant on file writes the
  day first. Reports say a date came out with day and month swapped: 8 June became 6 August.
  Any channel, every tenant. This is what #4471 describes.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, people type month
  first as told, and the form reads it day first. Only form reports, only from booking-v3
  tenants. The parser fix does not touch it.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, not by a swapped month ("Tuesday 6 October showed as
  Monday", "client in Lisbon got Tuesday, client in Toronto got Monday"). That points at a time
  zone shift, not at date order. The desk read these as a one-day move and still filed them under `parser`.

## Why #4471 looked like one problem

- Duplicates are merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  All three problems produce the same subjects: "Date is wrong", "Wrong day on the invite".
- The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser; it disagrees with the tenant's form
  in both directions. Count by tenant and form, not by tag.
- The ticket export repeats reports across pages. Count each id once.

